N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition; this is the compositional audit. Every bar totals eight eighths with no pickup. Calls (bars 1, 3, 9): A4 and C5 quarters, a D5-D5-C5-A4 sixteenth group on beat 3, then C5 (bars 1, 9) or G4 (bar 3) on beat 4; bar 11 varies it as C5-D5 quarters, C5-A4-G4-E4 sixteenths and G4. Answers (bars 2, 4, 10): A4-G4-A4 or E4-G4-A4 quarters and a quarter rest; bar 12 closes A4-G4 quarters and an A4 half. Middle section (bars 5-8): E4-G4 quarters, A4 half | G4-E4, D4 half | E4-G4-A4-C5 | D5-C5 quarters, A4 half. Pitch set A-C-D-E-G, range D4-D5, largest leap a fourth (E4 to A4, G4 to C5), no syncopation, dots or ties; every sixteenth group fills one whole beat. Form ABA (calls, quiet middle, calls). Originality check: compared with "Allah Ho Allah Ho" (not used: no text, melodic phrase or rhythm cycle) and with common call-and-response school songs (Funga Alafia, Tongo, Che Che Koolay, Down by the Bay, If You're Happy and You Know It); the "who will ... along with me" question and la-so-la answer are common property and match none of them.
N:UNCERTAIN: The key reads as Grade 4 in the analyzer because the curriculum names minor tonality in Grade 4; the melody is la-pentatonic, which Grade 3 meets as low la, and a teacher should confirm that reading. Whether a secular piece should be paired with this devotional recording at all, and how, is for a qawwali-informed educator to decide; tempo, the sixteenth group and the leader's improvised action word need a classroom trial.
